---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import JpgToWebp from '@/components/tools/image-convert/JpgToWebp.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="jpg-to-webp">
  <JpgToWebp client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert JPG to WebP</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop your JPG or JPEG files above. To optimise a site, drop the whole folder at once.</li>
      <li>Set the quality slider. 80 is the standard start point. The before and after size of each file shows if you can go lower.</li>
      <li>Compare the numbers. Each file shows its original size, its WebP size and the percentage change, so you can find the rare image that gets larger.</li>
      <li>Download the files one by one, or all of them as one ZIP.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How much you actually save</h2>
    <p>
      The common figure for JPG to WebP is "25–34% smaller than JPEG", from Google's own study of libwebp
      against libjpeg at matched SSIM. That number is fairly accurate in practice, but the spread is wide and
      depends almost fully on image content:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th align="left">Content</th>
            <th align="left">Typical saving at matched quality</th>
            <th align="left">Notes</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Skies, gradients, studio backdrops</td><td>35–50%</td><td>Smooth regions are where WebP is strongest</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Portraits and product shots</td><td>25–35%</td><td>The typical case; matches the headline figure</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Landscape with dense foliage</td><td>10–20%</td><td>High-frequency detail is expensive in any codec</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Film grain or high-ISO noise</td><td>0–10%, occasionally larger</td><td>Noise is incompressible; check the before/after figures</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Already heavily compressed JPEG</td><td>varies</td><td>Re-encoding artefacts costs bits. Raise quality to 85</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>About re-encoding a lossy file</h2>
    <p>
      JPG to WebP decodes one lossy image and encodes it with a second lossy codec. In theory that adds damage.
      In practice there is no problem at quality 80 or above, because the JPEG artefacts are low-contrast and
      WebP spends very few bits on them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Repeated round trips do matter. If you have the original camera file or the export from your editor,
      convert that to WebP, not an already-compressed web JPEG. One lossy generation is always better than two.
    </p>

    <h2>Using WebP on a real website</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Serve WebP with a JPEG fallback through a picture element, or let your CDN negotiate on the Accept header. All current browsers accept WebP, but the fallback costs nothing.</li>
      <li>Keep your JPEG originals. WebP is a delivery format, not an archive format.</li>
      <li>Resize before you compress. A 4000 px photo reduced to the 1200 px it displays at saves many more bytes than any codec choice.</li>
      <li>Set explicit width and height attributes so the layout does not shift while images load.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For smaller files, <a href="/image-to-avif">AVIF</a> is usually another 20–30% below WebP, with much slower
      encoding. In the other direction, <a href="/webp-to-jpg">WebP to JPG</a> gives compatibility with older
      software, and the <a href="/image-converter">universal image converter</a> handles mixed batches.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
